- [Test helpers](https://github.com/tauri-apps/tauri/blob/9452ddee5ebefd9b678a94ff003521379df6c9ae/crates/tauri/src/test/mod.rs): IPC helpers exist; no registered-protocol request helper.

Searched existing issues for MockRuntime/protocol, custom protocol/test, and mock/scheme. #8199 concerns exporting MockRuntime, which is already exposed, rather than protocol dispatch. Our application's current workaround separately proves builder registration through the IPC local-origin check and drives the same production handler through a captured request/responder boundary. That does not prove live WebKit dispatch.

### Describe the problem

On Windows, WebView2 handles browser keyboard shortcuts itself: Ctrl+F opens a find bar, F5 and Ctrl+R reload the page, Ctrl+P prints it, Ctrl+U opens a view-source window, and Ctrl+Shift+I or F12 open the developer tools. In a desktop app these are almost never wanted: a reload throws away the app's state and Ctrl+U opens a separate WebView2 window that Tauri does not control.

WebView2 has a single setting that turns all of these off while still passing the keys to the page: [`ICoreWebView2Settings3::put_AreBrowserAcceleratorKeysEnabled`](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-edge/webview2/reference/winrt/microsoft_web_webview2_core/corewebview2settings#arebrowseracceleratorkeysenabled). wry has supported it since December 2022 ([wry#792](https://github.com/tauri-apps/wry/pull/792)) as `WebViewBuilderExtWindows::with_browser_accelerator_keys`. In wry 0.55.1 it is applied in `src/webview2/mod.rs`:

```rust
if !pl_attrs.browser_accelerator_keys {
  if let Ok(settings3) = settings.cast::<ICoreWebView2Settings3>() {
    settings3.SetAreBrowserAcceleratorKeysEnabled(false)?;
  }
}
```

Tauri never passes it through. Neither tauri 2.11.5, tauri-runtime-wry 2.11.4 nor tauri-utils 2.9.3 mentions `browser_accelerator_keys` anywhere, so it is not in `WebviewWindowBuilder`, `WebviewBuilder` or the window config. The neighbouring WebView2-only options are all passed through in tauri-runtime-wry (`src/lib.rs`, around lines 5056 to 5080): `additional_browser_args`, `scroll_bar_style` and `browser_extensions_enabled`.

#7418 asks for a way to turn off browser shortcuts in general. It has been open since July 2023, and the answers there are a `with_webview` workaround and blocking keys in JavaScript. The option has existed in wry for the whole time; it only needs exposing.

### Describe the solution you'd like

The same treatment as `browser_extensions_enabled`:

- `WebviewWindowBuilder::browser_accelerator_keys(enabled: bool)` and `WebviewBuilder::browser_accelerator_keys(enabled: bool)`, documented as Windows-only, defaulting to `true` (WebView2's own default, so nothing changes for existing apps);
- a `browserAcceleratorKeys` field in the window config (`app.windows[]`), passed to wry's `with_browser_accelerator_keys` in tauri-runtime-wry.

### Alternatives considered

- **Setting it after the window exists, through `with_webview`.** This works, but every app has to add `webview2-com` and `windows-core` as Windows-only dependencies at exactly the versions wry uses (0.38 and 0.61 today), and update them whenever Tauri moves to a new wry. It also only reaches windows that exist at that point. What we use now:

  ```rust
  #[cfg(windows)]
  if let Some(window) = app.get_webview_window("main") {
      use webview2_com::Microsoft::Web::WebView2::Win32::ICoreWebView2Settings3;
      use windows_core::Interface;
      let _ = window.with_webview(|webview| unsafe {
          if let Ok(settings) = webview.controller().CoreWebView2().and_then(|w| w.Settings()) {
              if let Ok(s3) = settings.cast::<ICoreWebView2Settings3>() {
                  let _ = s3.SetAreBrowserAcceleratorKeysEnabled(false);
              }
          }
      });
  }
  ```

- **Calling `preventDefault()` on `keydown` in JavaScript.** This needs a list of every browser shortcut, and it gets in the way of the app's own native menu accelerators that use the same keys, such as F5 for an app-level Refresh.

### Additional context

wry's `with_default_context_menus` (WebView2's `AreDefaultContextMenusEnabled`) is not exposed by Tauri either, and could be added the same way.

Versions: tauri 2.11.5, tauri-runtime-wry 2.11.4, tauri-utils 2.9.3, wry 0.55.1.

